4. **What demo-pk runs, per the deploy records (UNVALIDATED today; the box is the source).** Demo round d15 (Seat D 69th, 2026-11-16) put `eb27110fc6e4` on demo, 27 of 27 swaps, REVISION head `eb27110fc6e4` (history.md around line 1867). Demo round d16 (Seat D 76th, 2026-11-20, history.md line 1174) moved only nine services to `d4ffc8308249` (staking, security, analytics, mcp-server, transfer, originate, vc-issuer, auth, api-gateway): **anchoring was NOT in d16's list**; the record says "KS-2196's fix was already on demo since d15". So Stuart's "demo-pk runs `d4ffc8308249`" is true of d16's nine services; **anchoring's own image is the d15 build, `eb27110fc6e4`, if that record is right.** Read the REVISION head, anchoring's image id and StartedAt, and compare to the deploy records; **do NOT trust the image's OCI revision LABEL** (AA 6th wrote that demo's anchoring "runs image revision `e02e15811b82`"; Seat D 69th measured that as a stale LABEL while the running id was `898567dc3687`, the d14 build; history.md around line 1842).
5. **The row's shape in code (drafter read at `d4ffc8308249`, `Blockchain/Dev/services/anchoring/src/`; you re-read at demo's actual running commit).** `anchor_store` has ONE row per `(document_id, network)`. `'failed'` is TERMINAL by construction (KS-535): `reconciler.ts` selects only `status IN ('pending','submitted','submitting')` (lines 233 and 304 at that sha), so **no reconciler pass picks a FAILED row up**; `anchorSubmission.ts` lines 1119 to 1160 show the in-process retry (`maxRetries`, backoff) only for the attempt that failed, and a service restart does not re-queue. There is **no admin or requeue route** in `index.ts` at that sha (routes: health, `/api/anchors`, batch, own-create, `:id`, `document/:documentId`, thread-mint, thread-state, `tx/:txHash`, verify, `/api/anchoring/submit` which is a MOCK, `queue` which is a READ). **The one path that re-arms a FAILED row is `POST /api/anchors` for the same document:** `dbPersistAnchor` (`index.ts` line 1537 on) does `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (document_id, network) DO UPDATE SET status = EXCLUDED.status, transaction_hash, ..., error_message, retry_count = EXCLUDED.retry_count, ...` with **no terminal-state guard**, then the handler calls `processAnchor(persistedId)` (line 2041). So a re-POST resets the row to `pending`, retry_count 0, transaction_hash NULL, and starts a fresh cycle. **Three hazards of that path:** (i) the upsert is UNCONDITIONAL, so the same POST sent against a row whose tx has landed would reset a CONFIRMED row's hash and status (and pay a second fee): hence Phase 0 (b) is a gate, not a courtesy; (ii) the route sits behind `jwtAuthenticate()` (`index.ts` line 487) and the ownership guard `mayWriteAnchorRows` (KS-1603: the caller's tenant must own the document), so it needs a valid caller: the connector (API key) credential Platform S uses, or an internal-emitter HMAC (`EMITTER_SIGNING_SECRET`, KS-1717). **You hold neither, and using a credential is a hold (below).** (iii) Seat AA 6th measured that Platform S's own polls re-POST the anchor and start a fresh three-attempt cycle (06:34, 06:42, 06:54 on 2026-11-13), while Stuart's 2026-11-24 comment says S's six-hourly revisits "are reads" and "nothing on S's side will re-send it": **a conflict, UNVALIDATED.** The demo box's anchoring log (the `POST /api/anchors` request lines by document, since 2026-11-13) answers which is true; if S does re-POST on its own, the cheapest and safest "re-submit" is S's own next poll, which the seat cannot cause and must not impersonate.
6. **The money.** The re-submit spends from demo's OWN platform wallet on the PREVIEW network (test ADA; KS-535: demo and Kintsugi never share a wallet). Fee for one anchor is on the order of 0.2 tADA (the node's expected figure for this anchor was 244,248 lovelace; UNVALIDATED for today's wallet). Treat it as money-class anyway: it needs the GO.
7. **Seat AA 6th's draft reply to Stuart, as a worked example of the wording:** `/Volumes/DevMASTER/!CODING/Secuura/Blockchain/5_Project_History/2026-11-15_seatAA-6th/drafts/stuart_reply_v2.md` (plain language, facts and labelled guesses). Read it; your reply is shorter and says only what you measured.

